Falsifies `paging::sv39::the_indices_and_offset_tile_the_address`.

The defect: ten bits of stride between levels against a nine-bit index field, so the fields no
longer abut. Between each pair of levels one address bit is now covered by no index at all, and
above the top level nine bits of the VA are simply dropped. The stride and the field width are
the same number for a reason (a 4 KiB table of 8-byte entries holds exactly 2^9 of them), and
writing them as two independent constants is what lets them disagree.

Expected to fail: `assert_eq!(reconstructed, va & 0x0000_007f_ffff_ffff)`, which rebuilds the
address from the three indices at the literal shifts 30, 21 and 12. Any `va` with bit 21 set
suffices: under the defect that bit belongs to no field.

**The index math is shared and the proof is not.** The `aarch64` record widens the offset and
the `x86_64` one reverses the level order; three ISAs prove one default method three ways.

diff --git a/crates/paging/src/lib.rs b/crates/paging/src/lib.rs
--- a/crates/paging/src/lib.rs
+++ b/crates/paging/src/lib.rs
@@ -477,7 +477,7 @@ pub trait PageFormat {
     /// covers the highest translated bits; each lower level shifts down by 9. Derived from
     /// [`LEVELS`](Self::LEVELS) so it is right for both a 4- and a 3-level walk.
     fn index(va: u64, level: usize) -> usize {
-        let shift = 9 * (Self::LEVELS - 1 - level) as u32 + 12;
+        let shift = 10 * (Self::LEVELS - 1 - level) as u32 + 12;
         ((va >> shift) & 0x1ff) as usize
     }
 
